From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 14:44:34 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: tas2764: keep SDZ high across system sleep

The codec suspend pulled the SDZ line low and the resume raised it again.
On boards where all amps share one SDZ supply (J616s: six SN012776 on one
fixed regulator) the last suspend puts every amp into hardware shutdown,
and the first resume puts every amp through a power-on reset. A few
milliseconds after the register cache sync, soc_resume_deferred() unmutes
the amps of a stream that was running at suspend, with the audio already
flowing.

After such a resume the woofer amps' I/V sense reports a negative average
power (-0.012 to -0.021 W) for several seconds; speakersafetyd treats that
as broken sense data and panics, the speaker volume lock times out, and the
speakers cut out until the daemon restarts (2-3 times per resume). It never
happens on cold boot or on any ordinary stream start: there the amps leave
software shutdown, not a power-on reset that is milliseconds old.

The power cycle also loses state the cache cannot restore: the Apple quirk
writes to page 0xfd sit in the volatile range, so regcache_sync() never
replays them, and after the first sleep the amps run without them until
reboot.

Leave SDZ (supply and per-amp GPIO) as it is across system sleep. The amp
waits in software shutdown, as between streams, and keeps its registers.
The cache-only window and the sync stay, so the bus is not touched while
suspended and a supply the platform cuts anyway is still restored.

Signed-off-by: Jonathan Goyvaerts <jonathan.goyvaerts@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: LLM
(cherry picked from commit e5c4c76f4169478ff11f5b80e77b26a887c9acf3)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c | 25 +++++++------------------
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 18 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
index 080c4f4caf..7a213baafe 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
@@ -149,6 +149,13 @@ static int tas2764_update_pwr_ctrl(struct tas2764_priv *tas2764)
 }
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_PM
+/*
+ * System sleep leaves the SDZ line high: the amp waits in software shutdown, as it does between
+ * streams, and keeps its registers. Pulling SDZ low here meant a power-on reset at resume, and
+ * soc_resume_deferred() unmutes the amps of a running stream a few milliseconds later: their I/V
+ * sense then read a negative average power for several seconds, and the uncached page-0xfd quirk
+ * writes were lost. After cold boot no amp is unmuted that fresh out of reset.
+ */
 static int tas2764_codec_suspend(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 {
 	struct tas2764_priv *tas2764 = snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(component);
@@ -161,11 +168,6 @@ static int tas2764_codec_suspend(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return ret;
 
-	if (tas2764->sdz_gpio)
-		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(tas2764->sdz_gpio, 0);
-
-	regulator_disable(tas2764->sdz_reg);
-
 	regcache_cache_only(tas2764->regmap, true);
 	regcache_mark_dirty(tas2764->regmap);
 
@@ -179,19 +181,6 @@ static int tas2764_codec_resume(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 	struct tas2764_priv *tas2764 = snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(component);
 	int ret;
 
-	ret = regulator_enable(tas2764->sdz_reg);
-
-	if (ret) {
-		dev_err(tas2764->dev, "Failed to enable regulator\n");
-		return ret;
-	}
-
-	if (tas2764->sdz_gpio) {
-		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(tas2764->sdz_gpio, 1);
-	}
-
-	usleep_range(1000, 2000);
-
 	regcache_cache_only(tas2764->regmap, false);
 
 	ret = regcache_sync(tas2764->regmap);
